'''Kitsai''' (also '''Kichai''') is an extinct member of the [[Caddoan language family]].<ref>Sturtevant and Fogelson, 616</ref> The French first record the [[Kichai people]]'s presence along the upper [[Red River of the South|Red River]] in 1701.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://accessgenealogy.com/louisiana/kichai-tribe.htm|title=Kichai Tribe|website=Access Genealogy|date=July 9, 2011 |language=en-US|access-date=2019-12-16}}</ref> By the 1840s Kitsai was spoken in southern [[Oklahoma]], but by 1940 no native speakers remained. It is thought to be most closely related to [[Pawnee language|Pawnee]].<ref>Sturtevant and Fogelson, 68</ref><ref>{{Ethnologue16|kii}} (retrieved 3 May 2010)</ref> The Kichai people today are enrolled in the [[Wichita (tribe)|Wichita and Affiliated Tribes (Wichita, Keechi)]], [[Waco tribe|Waco]] and [[Tawakonie]]), headquartered in [[Anadarko, Oklahoma]].

