==Documentation==
Kitsai is documented in the still mostly-unpublished field notes of anthropologist [[Alexander Lesser]], of [[Hofstra University]]. Lesser discovered five speakers of Kitsai in 1928 and 1929, none of whom spoke English. Communicating to the Kitsai speakers through Wichita/English bilingual translators, he filled 41 notebooks with Kitsai material.<ref name=bucca>{{cite journal |first1=Salvador |last1=Bucca |first2=Alexander |last2=Lesser |jstor=1263879 |title=Kitsai Phonology and Morphophonemics |page=7 |journal=International Journal of American Linguistics |volume=35 |number=1 |date=January 1969|doi=10.1086/465034 |s2cid=143469230 }}</ref>

Kai Kai was the last fluent speaker of Kitsai. She was born around 1849 and lived eight miles north of Anadarko. Kai Kai worked with Lesser to record vocabulary and [[oral tradition|oral history]] and prepare a grammar of the language.<ref>{{cite magazine |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080310001009/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,743877,00.html |url=http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,743877,00.html |archive-date=2008-03-10 |title=Science: Last of the Kitsai |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |date=27 June 1932 |access-date=3 May 2010}}</ref>

In the 1960s, Lesser shared his materials with [[Salvador Bucca]] of the [[Universidad Nacional de Buenos Aires]], and they published scholarly articles on Kitsai.<ref name=bucca/>

